{"skill":{"slug":"faster-whisper","displayName":"Faster Whisper"},"version":{"version":"1.5.1","createdAt":1771432097764,"changelog":"- Fixed --skip-existing in multi-format mode to check ALL format outputs before skipping\n- Fixed --no-timestamps conflict check missing lrc, ass, ttml formats\n- Fixed --speaker-names silently doing nothing without --diarize; now prints a warning\n- Batch summary now shows skipped file count when --skip-existing is active","changelogSource":"user","license":null,"files":[{"path":"SKILL.md","size":67024,"sha256":"970204a5eac1b70195b2f1835b1d3bacb4341e327968549e7753f16a9e0653bf","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"requirements.txt","size":117,"sha256":"db500dda68105d00efa2be011ba9beb7efa27a181d13cbb024eb671ecca7297d","contentType":"text/plain"},{"path":"scripts/transcribe.py","size":114264,"sha256":"3c313601d0aa3586eb63e6e8dd45fdca65481151fdf247aee07db8da3dbef32a","contentType":"text/plain"},{"path":"setup.sh","size":12721,"sha256":"a678dfd560c09e8084e0fcc84c285d775d31365ce2dcbb51daf808dc48179870","contentType":"application/x-sh"},{"path":"skill.json","size":599,"sha256":"bffcbb78c3010933dd97bb6606b876108ddd2d8c69e426c7c4a8cf4efdc42974","contentType":"application/json"},{"path":"skill-card.md","size":2969,"sha256":"b7df373c7c0f34453e90cf5c39d20a78551c187740971ced879e3504459e31e6","contentType":"text/markdown"}],"security":{"status":"suspicious","hasWarnings":true,"checkedAt":1789022906589,"model":null,"hasScanResult":true,"sha256hash":"6d3042db6ceec6aef70d80af104ef98b1f046d7709ebf93b73f9acbf659a017c","virustotalUrl":"https://www.virustotal.com/gui/file/6d3042db6ceec6aef70d80af104ef98b1f046d7709ebf93b73f9acbf659a017c","scanners":{"vt":{"status":"suspicious","verdict":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","analysis":"Type: OpenClaw Skill\nName: faster-whisper\nVersion: 1.5.1\n\nThis skill is classified as suspicious due to its broad capabilities, which include downloading content from arbitrary URLs via `yt-dlp`, executing `ffmpeg` for audio/video processing and subtitle burning, and performing self-updates of its core dependency. While these actions are plausibly aligned with the stated purpose of a comprehensive transcription tool, they grant significant access to the network and local file system. The `SKILL.md` agent guidance does not contain any malicious prompt injection attempts, and the `setup.sh` and `scripts/transcribe.py` files implement these powerful features using `subprocess.run()` with argument lists, which mitigates direct shell injection vulnerabilities. However, the inherent power of these operations, even when used for legitimate purposes, elevates the risk profile beyond a purely benign classification.","source":"palm","checkedAt":1779160208617},"skillspector":{"status":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","score":100,"severity":"CRITICAL","recommendation":"DO_NOT_INSTALL","issueCount":28,"checkedAt":1789022906589},"llm":{"status":"suspicious","verdict":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","confidence":"high","summary":"This transcription skill is useful and mostly transparent, but it needs Review because it can fetch arbitrary network media, use Hugging Face credentials, and update/install unpinned code without tight safeguards.","dimensions":[{"detail":"Local file reads/writes, ffmpeg and yt-dlp subprocesses, remote media retrieval, model cache access, and optional Hugging Face token access fit the transcription purpose but are broad and not strongly contained, especially for arbitrary URLs and RSS enclosure URLs.","label":"Credentials","name":"environment_proportionality","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"Setup creates a local virtual environment and installs expected ML dependencies, but dependencies are lower-bound or unpinned, optional packages are unconstrained, update paths pull latest packages, and the Docker example uses a mutable latest-cuda image tag.","label":"Install Mechanism","name":"install_mechanism","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"The skill often tells the agent to add risky flags only when explicitly requested, but it lacks strong confirmation or destination-scope rules for URL/RSS downloads, Hugging Face token use, in-place updates, and server-mode examples.","label":"Instruction Scope","name":"instruction_scope","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"No backdoor, scheduled task, or hidden service persistence was found; persistence is mainly expected caches, output files, a skill venv, optional kept temp files, and user-invoked dependency updates.","label":"Persistence & Privilege","name":"persistence_privilege","rating":"note"},{"detail":"The core speech-to-text purpose is coherent, and URL/RSS transcription, diarization, subtitle output, and batch processing are disclosed, but the local/offline framing under-discloses network fetching, model downloads, credential use, and generated active HTML output.","label":"Purpose & Capability","name":"purpose_capability","rating":"concern"}],"guidance":"Install only if you are comfortable with a transcription tool that can contact external media sites, Hugging Face, package indexes, and optional Docker images. Avoid using it with private RSS/URLs or sensitive Hugging Face tokens unless you can isolate the environment, prefer pinned dependencies/images, and do not open generated HTML transcripts from untrusted audio without sanitizing them first.","findings":"[LP3] unexpected: The artifact documents many capabilities, but no explicit permission boundary or confirmation model is provided for higher-risk URL, token, update, and file-output paths.\n[SDI-1] unexpected: The SKILL.md frontmatter mentions URL/YouTube input, but the separate manifest description remains narrowly framed as local speech-to-text, so network behavior and credential-adjacent features are not equally visible everywhere.\n[SQP-2] expected: Remote URL/RSS fetching is an intended transcription feature, but the code accepts arbitrary destinations and RSS enclosure URLs without private-network filtering or clear privacy warnings.\n[SQP-1] unexpected: Some trigger phrases are broad for an agent router; the artifact does include guidance to use flags only when users ask, which downgrades this from malicious to a Review concern.\n[P9] expected: The whitespace finding corresponds to aligned markdown table formatting in the quick reference; I did not find hidden instructions or prompt-injection content there.\n[RP1] unexpected: The Docker server-mode example uses a mutable latest-cuda image tag, which is a supply-chain review issue rather than evidence of malicious behavior.\n[EA2] expected: The phrase about automatically removing silence describes transcription preprocessing behavior, not autonomous destructive user-data deletion.\n[AST4] expected: Subprocess use is mostly expected for ffmpeg, yt-dlp, and package-manager operations and generally uses argument lists rather than shell=True; the concern is broad execution surface, not command injection evidence.\n[SDI-2] unexpected: Runtime self-update of faster-whisper is disclosed, but it is not necessary for ordinary transcription and pulls unreviewed latest dependency code into the skill venv.\n[PE2] expected: The sudo matches are printed package-install suggestions for users, not commands the setup script executes automatically.\n[SC1] unexpected: The requirements file uses faster-whisper>=1.2.1, so installations and updates are not reproducible or pinned to reviewed versions.\n[T09] unexpected: The supplied --hf-token is copied into process-wide environment variables before later subprocesses may run, so unrelated child tools can inherit a sensitive token.\n[T08] unexpected: The dependency and update behavior creates supply-chain risk through unconstrained package installation, though no artifact evidence shows a malicious package.","agenticRiskFindings":null,"riskSummary":null,"model":null,"checkedAt":1789022906589}}}}}